WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning Hank, it's Monday.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:05.290
I woke up on Friday around 8 AM in Los Angeles,
the city of palm trees, pleasant weather,

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:10.980
news helicopters, prestigious zip codes, terrible
traffic, and astonishingly sparse mobile internet.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.349
As I drove over to my friend Ransom's house
I heard on the radio there had been an earthquake

00:00:14.349 --> 00:00:18.260
and a tsunami in Japan, and I was so wrapped
up in the news that I got lost, and since

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:22.359
my phone didn't work as a mobile atlas anymore,
I had to call Ransom for directions. "I mean,

00:00:22.359 --> 00:00:25.550
I see a sign that says Wilshire and I see
a sign that says San Basante and the signs

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.430
seem to imply, uh, that there's a perpendicular
rather than parallel relationship between

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:33.110
those two places but what you're seeing on
the map disagrees with that."

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.980
I finally made it to his house, we went inside
and read about the earthquake and then somehow

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.340
got to talking about his photographs. Ransom
collects photographs taken of and by people

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.910
he doesn't know. He has thousands of them
in these plastic slip cases, and he's obsessed

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:49.280
with the stories that these photographs contain,
particularly when you flip them over. This

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.030
one, for instance, "Rock Wall near Rose Bowl
Pasadena, California, where Dorothy found

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:57.800
a baby girl on January 24, 1961."

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.840
Hank, Ransom used photographs like this one
to make one of the greatest youtube videos

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.780
of all time, link in the dooblydoo.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:06.520
We drove down to the beach then, where this
newscaster was like "This very ocean just

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.330
devastated Japan." And then I ran into the
water like a boss, forgetting that I had shoes

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:15.720
on. Then Randy took me over to the Venice
Canals, a few blocks of L.A. that look like

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.580
Southern California and Amsterdam had a baby,
and we walked around talking about how weird

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:25.290
it was to stroll these sun-drenched canals
on such a tragic day. But then really, all

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:29.750
human life in lived in close proximity to
tragedy. We saw bird flowers and a park for

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:34.020
ducks and these turtles. Too quiet for the
camera to hear, Ransom said "Wouldn't it be

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.030
sad if a child's pool were enough imprison
you?"

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:41.101
Then I had to leave for a wedding, so I participated
in an activity that people in Los Angeles

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:46.690
call "hopping on the freeway" which does not
in any way resemble hopping. As I idled back

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:51.659
toward our hotel, the news on the radio got
worse and worse. And then I put on a suit

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:52.659
and went to the wedding.

00:01:52.659 --> 00:01:56.550
The reception was at a beautiful home in Beverley
Hills, and as I drank free champagne and looked

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:00.299
out over the pool, I thought about how, even
though we were kind of in a desert, the water

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:04.799
had been everywhere that day. From the pool
to my champagne glass, from the turtle pond

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:08.950
to the canals to the ocean. I found myself
wishing there were some way for those of us

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:13.840
on one side of an ocean to tell people on
the other side that we were thinking of them,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.040
a way that I could say that, not only to the
people of Japan, but also to Dorothy, who

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:23.090
had found that baby girl in 1961. But images
can travel in a way that our thoughts can

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:29.930
not, and that night I felt sad and frustrated
that technology can never quite bridge the

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.250
oceans between us.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:34.760
Hank, you'll find a link to the Red Cross
disaster relief fund in the dooblydoo. Welcome

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.570
back from Haiti, I'll see you on Wednesday.

